 the Upper Rio\nGrande Valley.\n\n* WHEN...From late tonight through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\nRockslides will be possible in the mountainous areas. Highway 160\nand Highway 17 will likely be significantly impacted by this storm.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Heavy rainfall is expected to fall Sunday and Monday across the\narea, leading to elevated concerns for flooding in areas that\nhave recently received excessive moisture, or have recently\nexperienced flooding.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejos River and\nthe Rio Grande, including Del Norte and Mogote.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood http://cotrip.org","ends":"2026-11-02T12:00:00-06:00","severity":"Severe","areaDesc":"Upper Rio Grande Valley/Eastern San Juan Mountains Below 10000 Ft; Eastern San Juan Mountains Above 10000 Ft","id":"urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.c43837866a64e434d490f547f232ecfdd4c91f85.001.1","url":"https://api.weather.gov/alerts/urn:oid:2.49.0.1.840.0.c43837866a64e434d490f547f232ecfdd4c91f85.001.1"},{"event":"Flood Watch","headline":"Flood Watch issued October 31 at 5:48AM MDT until November 4 at 5:00AM MST by NWS Pueblo CO","description":"* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall will be possible,\nespecially over the western half of San Luis Valley.\n\n* WHERE...The western half of the San Luis Valley, including Del\nNorte, Antonito and Mogote.\n\n* WHEN...From Sunday afternoon through late Tuesday night.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of rivers,\ncreeks, streams, and other low-lying and flood-prone location.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Runoff from heavy rain and melting snow over the San Juan\nMountains may cause flooding in the San Luis Valley, especially\nMonday and Tuesday as high flows move out of the mountains and\nthrough the Rio Grande and Conejos River.\n- The greatest risk of flooding will be along the Conejo